{-
   The other half of `BorrowRefill.yana` - the hole nothing fills.

   # resolver: ownership

   Taking a value out of borrowed storage is a loan of two halves, and only the second one is a
   check: the caller may look into its own storage again the moment this frame returns, so that is
   the point at which the storage has to hold something. Each function below empties `target` and
   then fails to put something back, in the three shapes the dataflow can tell apart.

   `partly` is refused with the same message as `never`, and deliberately: a refill on one path and
   not on another leaves the caller holding a value only a run-time bit knows about, and a drop flag
   is not an answer here - a flag says whether to *release*, and what is missing is a value.

   `Reject.BorrowAlias.yana` is the third half, split off for the reason `Reject.CopyInstance.yana`
   is: resolution stops before the analysis runs, so a fixture holding both would only ever report
   one of them.
-}

fn eat(->s: String) -> Int = truncate(length(s)) :: Int

fn never(&target: String) -> Int:
    return eat(target)

fn partly(&target: String, flag: Bool) -> Int:
    if flag then target = "put back" else eat(target)
    return 0

fn afterTheHole(&target: String) -> Int:
    let first = eat(target)
    return first + eat(target)

fn main() -> Int = 0
